sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-20> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Cellular automata whose information transmission is highly constrained so that patterns are essentially nested, and additive rules such as rules 90 and 150 whose states after a power-of-two number of steps are simple superpositions of shifted copies of the initial condition, cannot be universal.";
    dc:type "mathematical";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Nested-pattern and additive cellular automata cannot be universal";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 11, “The Notion of Computation”; section “The Threshold of Universality in Cellular Automata” (from p. 694), p. 696. Scope: additive and nested-pattern cellular automata, e.g. rules 90 and 150"
}
